WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.780
Hi, I'm Ashlee Vance,

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.913
and welcome to my living
room here in California.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.090
We've got about seven
rolls of toilet paper left

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.790
and two wonderful boys helping out,

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.320
filming me right over here.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.800
We obviously can't do Hello
World like we normally would,

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.530
so this is the from home version.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:37.810
We thought we'd try to take
this challenge as an opportunity

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:42.190
to take all our viewers on
kind of a trip around the world

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.140
to see what different
inventors and scientists

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.150
and technologists are up to.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.350
The first place we're gonna
head to is New Zealand.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.240
I've got a friend there
named Michelle Dickinson,

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.404
she's a scientist who has been out front

00:00:55.404 --> 00:00:56.910
talking about the virus.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:00.403
So, off we go to New Zealand
first to meet Michelle.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.190
My name is Dr. Michelle Dickinson

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.290
and I am a nanotechnologist
having worked in the tech sector

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.520
for 20 years around the world.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.040
And a couple of years ago I
decided to quit my hardware job

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.420
and move to helping
educate kids and parents

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.410
about science and technology,
and get more kids involved

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.598
who might not see themselves
as scientists or engineers.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.500
We were a company who
traveled around the world

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.070
with our live theater shows

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.333
and our school teacher training programs.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:33.817
And then that died.

00:01:33.817 --> 00:01:35.300
So, yay.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.670
Thanks, COVID.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.280
So, this hits, and your
business has to change.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.800
And so, what have you done?

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.020
How have you adapted on the fly?

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:43.853
We said to our team,

00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:46.120
"What do people need in this lockdown?"

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.010
And as we saw schools were closing,

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:49.587
we spoke to teachers and they said,

00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:51.227
"We've never done online teaching before.

00:01:51.227 --> 00:01:52.520
"We don't know what that is."

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.930
And they said, "The thing we're
gonna drop first is STEM,"

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.070
so science and technology.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.480
They said, "That's too
hard for us to do online."

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.107
I just said, "Well, this is what we do.

00:02:00.107 --> 00:02:03.287
"How can we help provide something to kids

00:02:03.287 --> 00:02:05.710
"to help them build their
confidence in STEM?"

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.110
Hi, I'm NanoGirl and I want to be a...

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:11.960
Superhero!

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.660
And so, we developed an online platform

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.120
that basically helps kids build
their science superpowers.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.720
This week, the superpower
I'm trying to get

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.570
is the power to fight
baddies like viruses, yuck.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.730
I saw some of the first
lessons were about COVID,

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.210
what is it, and making
your own special soap

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:31.193
to deal with it.

00:02:31.193 --> 00:02:32.807
It's so simple, but parents were like,

00:02:32.807 --> 00:02:34.407
"My kids won't wash their hands.

00:02:34.407 --> 00:02:35.709
"I need to motivate them."

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:37.457
We said, "Well, if you
make the Superpower Soap

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:39.227
"where it's got that
superhero sticker in it,

00:02:39.227 --> 00:02:42.330
"they'll get super powers every
time they touch this soap."

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.920
And parents have said literally
the kids love their soap,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.000
they're washing their hands all the time

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.340
to get super powers.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.140
And it just helped to motivate them.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.680
Look at that, we destroyed our baddie,

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.370
the virus is destroyed.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:57.070
You are the superhero.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:00.620
Our kids, actually they've
sort of accepted all this

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:03.130
really well, I feel like,
but it is, it's nuts.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.020
And you're lucky Ashlee, right,
'cause you're tech savvy.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.077
But there are millions of
parents around the world

00:03:07.077 --> 00:03:09.460
who science and tech wasn't their thing,

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:12.000
and they go back to their
13-year-old fearful self

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.880
of a Bunsen burner and a Petri
dish when we talk about it.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.870
And so, we're all about how
do we empower those people,

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.780
those people who haven't
touched science for 40 years,

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:24.370
maybe, to be able to have these
conversations about viruses.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.820
And how you transfer it,
and what they actually are,

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.130
and what they could do
without it being too fearful.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.210
Because we understand
when we're talking to kids

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.820
we've got to help them
understand that it's serious,

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.100
but also not induce so much anxiety

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.207
that they become petrified of everything.

00:03:38.207 --> 00:03:40.150
So how do people find this?

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:43.240
So nanogirllabs.com is
how you basically find it.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.550
We're on all of the channels,
social, you name it.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.520
Destroy the virus baddies, yay!

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:50.580
You can look at our free stuff,

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.780
and if you want to subscribe you can.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:52.613
If you can't afford to,

00:03:52.613 --> 00:03:55.200
just press the I can't
afford to do this button

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.010
and we'll try and give you free access.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.360
As some of the lockdowns started,

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.800
I caught you on the Internet

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.140
with the prime minister in New Zealand.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:08.460
It seemed like you were
educating people about COVID.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:09.520
Yeah, so luckily,

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.790
I guess New Zealand has been
slightly behind the COVID curve

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:15.900
in that we've got to watch
other countries do their thing

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.710
and use a lot of data.

00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:18.720
We have a prime minister

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.160
who has worked really
hard on communication.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.070
She's brought me into that team
to communicate to the nation

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.510
about what is COVID, what should we do,

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:28.070
how can we help each other.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:29.580
Lots of people getting questions around,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.650
how to explain what this is to children?

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:34.210
So, the easiest way to explain it,

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:37.070
is if your child has had the
common cold, for example,

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.120
they would have had a
virus that had a dangly bit

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.460
that attaches to our nose.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:42.580
And it would have stuck to
our nose and made them sick.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.130
The Covid-19 we're talking about,

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.030
it has a different dangly bit,
and it attaches to our lungs.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.900
One of the things we've done
really well in New Zealand,

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.640
I think, is set up an alert
system pretty early that said,

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.450
here's the alert system, it's four levels,

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:55.630
and here's where we are now,

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:58.240
and this is what moving to the
next levels will look like.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.593
That allowed everybody to prepare early.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:04.220
We're on a lockdown, but
everybody seems to be playing ball

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:05.890
and our supermarkets are full of food

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:07.060
'cause nobody panic-bought

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:09.200
'cause they knew what
the message was early on.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:10.470
You locked down super early

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.540
before there was kind of hardly any cases.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.550
And so this was just
about wanting to be what,

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.470
just incredibly proactive on this

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.010
and sort of get ahead of things?

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.720
Yeah, and communication has been great.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.235
We haven't just communicated
it to those who watch politics.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.610
We did a press conference just
for children in parliament.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.200
That was really important
because obviously children

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.010
are a big part of this as
well as everybody else.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:38.710
So, our job as superheros

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:40.940
is to stop the virus from spreading.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.210
And we wanted to include
them in the conversation,

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.070
so that they were also
gonna take part in this

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:46.690
and understand their role.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:52.290
As a scientist, how close
attention are you paying

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:55.250
to how this is spreading
and the treatments,

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:58.540
and is this something that
you're really following deeply?

00:05:58.540 --> 00:05:59.870
Look, I'm a nanotechnologist,

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:03.320
and a virus is literally
as nanotech as you get.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.060
So, I'm interested for many reasons.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.420
One, I'm interested as a
communicator as to how to explain

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:10.170
the world of the tiny and the invisible,

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.770
which is what I've done my
whole life to everybody else,

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:15.210
'cause I can understand how
it might seem really scary

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.770
that there's this invisible pathogen

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.530
that might be fatal to us.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.770
I think our scientific
literacy as a population

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:23.810
has decreased over the past 10 years

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:25.790
as we haven't focused on science.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:27.430
And so I'm just focused on making sure

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:30.440
that some of these complex
medical journal publications

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:33.610
are actually explained
in a way that people can,

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.770
help them to understand
their life at home.

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.310
What can I do to control my environment

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:40.850
and my bubble to keep my family safe?

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:45.000
The US, we have this
explosion in cases right now,

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.690
but I am deeply curious about how the US

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:49.793
is being perceived overseas.

00:06:50.746 --> 00:06:53.000
Well, I think different
countries are dealing with it

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.770
in different ways, and I think
that's pretty sad actually

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.160
because this is a worldwide virus.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:00.890
The virus is apolitical,

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:02.990
and it doesn't care
about countries' borders.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:04.350
And I think this is really a time

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:05.860
when we should be pulling together

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.670
and not trying something
out and seeing what happens.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:10.400
If there's inspiration in any of this,

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:15.280
it seems to be the way
science has come through

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.080
all over the world, how
quickly this got sequenced,

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.550
how quickly we're working
on detection, on vaccines.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:25.890
Is there gonna be some
good that comes out of this

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:27.070
a year from now?

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:27.903
Look, I hope so.

00:07:27.903 --> 00:07:29.710
I'm also seeing some bad coming out of it.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:31.640
I think the lack of scientific literacy

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:34.370
means everybody believes
everything that's coming in

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:36.590
and not able to have that
scientific literacy to go,

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:37.940
is this a good or a bad idea?

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:39.400
How do I look this up?

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:40.850
I've talked to a couple of companies.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.070
They've got vaccines and animal trials

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:45.527
and they're telling me,
"Look, we know this is safe,

00:07:45.527 --> 00:07:47.957
"if we don't have to go
through the arduous process,

00:07:47.957 --> 00:07:50.310
"we could get this to
people much more quickly."

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:52.030
So, the thing with a vaccine,

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:53.820
and anytime you modify
something in the body,

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.310
you are literally changing
a person's immune system.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:58.850
And that has two outcomes.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:01.720
The great outcome is it
makes you hopefully protected

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:04.360
against what is this virus right now.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.910
But there's also an adverse
reaction in that we don't know

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.130
how it's gonna affect your
immune system in a different way.

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:11.760
So, what if we vaccinate
everybody against COVID,

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.640
but suddenly you're more
susceptible to something else.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.400
And that's why long-term clinical trials

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:17.840
become really important.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:20.223
It's those side effects
that we need to look at.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.140
Okay, this is Bowie.

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:25.320
This is Tucker.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:26.980
Hey, Bowie and Tucker.

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:30.410
Did you guys have any
questions about how New Zealand

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:31.650
is dealing with the virus?

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.990
Or about the virus, you know
Michelle is a scientist.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:37.070
How much on quarantine are you?

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.450
So, it's super serious here.

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.870
So, we have to stay at home

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:43.140
unless we are going to the supermarket,

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:44.850
and only one person from our home

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.210
is allowed to go to the supermarket,

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:47.870
you can't take everybody else,

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:50.180
or you're going to the
pharmacy to pick up drugs.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:53.210
You're allowed to go for
a small walk every day,

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:54.670
but that is it.

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:56.760
So, all of our online shopping is off.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.410
You cannot order anything online

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:00.370
unless it's medical or food.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:01.460
All the takeout's shut.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:04.130
So, everybody can only buy
food from the grocery store

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:05.730
and then has to cook it at home.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:08.840
Yeah, it's pretty strict.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:13.640
Well, I think we are going
to try to make one of your,

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:14.910
we're gonna go through
one of the experiments.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:16.340
I think we're gonna try to make the--

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:17.640
The catapult plane, it's my favorite,

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:18.880
make the catapult plane.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:20.097
We're gonna do that,

00:09:20.097 --> 00:09:22.160
and thanks so much for
spending time with us.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:23.363
Thank you, stay safe.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:29.610
Why don't we make a paper
plane that gets its thrust

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:34.560
and its power, not from an
engine, but from an elastic band.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.400
For this experiment,
you will need scissors,

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.213
tape, and a pencil.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:43.320
Start by measuring and
drawing a rectangle.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.570
Then, cut it out.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.540
Fold your paper back.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:50.430
Place your elastic band into that hook.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.050
This is gonna be our launch mechanism.

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:56.210
Next, you want to draw a
triangle on your piece of card.

00:09:56.210 --> 00:10:01.210
You're gonna tape this bit to
the underside of your plane.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:03.260
Now you're ready to launch.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:05.633
Three, two, one.

00:10:09.104 --> 00:10:10.271
Ow!

